export const POSTS = [
 {slug:`jensen-huang-nvidia-long-bets`,pillar:`Mind`,date:`2026-10-07`,read:7,
  sources:[`NVIDIA Corporation: company history and annual reports (nvidia.com)`,`NVIDIA Corporation, Form S-1 registration statement, US SEC, 1998–1999`,`NVIDIA: CUDA launch announcement, 2006`,`Krizhevsky A, Sutskever I, Hinton GE (2012). ImageNet classification with deep convolutional neural networks. NeurIPS`],
  title:`Jensen Huang and Nvidia: a near-failure, a long bet and patience`,
  lede:`Nvidia's chips now power much of the AI industry. Thirty years ago it nearly failed with its first product. The story is about surviving early mistakes and investing for years before the payoff.`,
  body:`<p><i>This story is based on publicly documented company history. Famous successes are rare; the useful part is the decisions and habits behind them.</i></p><h2>The background</h2><p>Jensen Huang was born in Taiwan in 1963 and moved to the United States as a child. As a teenager he worked at a Denny's restaurant, washing dishes and waiting tables. He studied electrical engineering at Oregon State University and later earned a master's degree at Stanford, then worked as a chip designer at LSI Logic and AMD.</p><h2>The start</h2><p>In 1993, Huang co-founded Nvidia with two engineers, Chris Malachowsky and Curtis Priem. They believed that video games and 3D graphics would need specialised chips. Much of the early planning reportedly happened over meals at a Denny's in San Jose.</p><h2>The near-failure</h2><p>Nvidia's first product, the NV1, launched in 1995 and was a commercial disappointment. It used a different technical approach from the one the industry, including Microsoft's new graphics standard, was moving towards. The company had limited money left and had to cut staff.</p><p>Nvidia changed direction and bet on the industry standard. Its RIVA 128 chip in 1997 sold well and saved the company. In 1999 it listed its shares publicly and launched the GeForce 256, which it marketed as the world's first GPU, or graphics processing unit.</p><h2>The long bet</h2><p>A GPU performs many simple calculations at the same time. Nvidia realised this could be useful far beyond games, for scientific research and complex data. In 2006 it launched CUDA, software that lets programmers use GPUs for general computing.</p><p>For years, this cost a lot of money with a limited payoff, and some investors questioned it. Then, in 2012, a team of researchers trained an image-recognition system called AlexNet on Nvidia GPUs, and it beat its rivals by a wide margin. That result helped spark the modern deep-learning boom. When AI demand surged in the 2020s, Nvidia's chips and its CUDA software were already widely used, and the company became one of the most valuable in the world.</p><h2>What is easy to misunderstand</h2><p>It is easy to say Nvidia "got lucky with AI". Timing certainly mattered, and nobody could predict exactly when AI would take off. But Nvidia had been building the tools that made it possible for more than a decade. Luck tends to favour those who are already prepared.</p><p>It is also worth noting the risks: Nvidia's success depends heavily on a few large customers and on fast-changing technology, and its share price has seen large falls as well as rises.</p><h2>The principle</h2><p><b>Admit a wrong turn quickly, then commit for the long term to the right one.</b></p><h2>A Pakistan angle</h2><p>Pakistan has a large and growing pool of young software developers and freelancers. Nvidia's story suggests that deep skills in a specific, technical area, kept up over many years, can become valuable in ways that are hard to predict. Learning fundamentals such as mathematics, programming and data, rather than chasing every trend, is a long bet of a similar kind.</p><h2>Three practical lessons</h2><ol><li><b>Early failure is common, not final.</b> Nvidia's first product failed; its response defined the company.</li><li><b>Follow where the market is going, not only your preferred approach.</b> Switching to the industry standard saved Nvidia.</li><li><b>Some investments take a decade to pay off.</b> CUDA was launched in 2006; its biggest payoff came many years later.</li></ol><h2>One action to try</h2><p>Choose one skill that will still matter in ten years. Write down a small weekly commitment to it, such as three hours, and track it for three months.</p><h2>Key idea</h2><p><b>Prepare for years, so you are ready when the moment arrives.</b></p>`},
 {slug:`jeff-bezos-amazon-long-term`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-10-07`,read:7,
  sources:[`Amazon.com, Inc., 1997 letter to shareholders`,`Amazon.com, Inc., Form S-1 registration statement, US SEC, 1997`,`Amazon.com, Inc., annual reports (profitability 2003; AWS launch 2006)`,`Amazon press release on Andy Jassy becoming CEO, July 2021`],
  title:`Jeff Bezos and Amazon: thinking in decades during a 90% share-price crash`,
  lede:`Amazon began as an online bookshop in a garage. Its share price later fell by more than 90% in the dot-com crash. The company survived by focusing on customers and the long term.`,
  body:`<p><i>This story is based on publicly documented company history. Amazon and Bezos have also faced significant criticism, for example over working conditions and market power. A success story is not a story without flaws.</i></p><h2>The decision</h2><p>In 1994, Jeff Bezos was a senior vice president at D.E. Shaw, a successful investment firm in New York. He noticed that internet use was growing extremely fast and decided to start an online business. He chose books because there were millions of titles, more than any physical shop could stock.</p><p>Leaving a secure, well-paid job was a hard decision. Bezos later described using what he called a "regret minimisation framework": imagining himself at 80 and asking which choice he would regret more. He decided that trying and failing would be easier to live with than never trying.</p><h2>The start</h2><p>Amazon was founded in July 1994 in a garage in Bellevue, near Seattle, and the website launched in 1995. Early staff packed books by hand. The company listed its shares in May 1997.</p><p>In his first letter to shareholders that year, Bezos wrote that Amazon would make decisions based on long-term market leadership rather than short-term profits. That letter became a reference point for how the company operated.</p><h2>The crash</h2><p>When the dot-com bubble burst in 2000 and 2001, Amazon's share price fell by more than 90% from its peak. Many internet companies of that era disappeared completely. Critics doubted Amazon would survive, and the company made layoffs and cut costs.</p><p>Amazon kept focusing on customers: wider selection, lower prices and faster delivery. It reported its first full-year profit in 2003.</p><h2>Building something new</h2><p>In 2006, Amazon launched Amazon Web Services, renting out computing power and storage to other businesses, using skills it had built to run its own website. AWS later became one of Amazon's most profitable businesses. Bezos stepped down as CEO in July 2021, handing over to Andy Jassy, who had led AWS.</p><h2>What is easy to misunderstand</h2><p>"Think long term" can sound like an excuse for losing money. For Amazon, it meant specific choices: reinvesting in infrastructure, focusing on measures that customers cared about, and accepting short-term criticism. Many companies that claimed to think long term during the dot-com boom simply ran out of money. Amazon survived partly because it had raised funds before the crash and cut costs when needed.</p><h2>The principle</h2><p><b>Decide what will still matter in ten years, and build on that.</b></p><h2>A Pakistan angle</h2><p>E-commerce in Pakistan has grown, but trust, delivery reliability and cash-on-delivery returns remain real challenges. Bezos's focus on things customers will always want, such as selection, fair prices and reliable delivery, is a useful lens for any Pakistani online seller, whether on Daraz, Instagram or a personal website.</p><h2>Three practical lessons</h2><ol><li><b>Use the regret test for big decisions.</b> Imagine looking back in 30 years and ask which choice you would regret more.</li><li><b>A falling price is not always a failing business,</b> but you must check the business, not just hope. Amazon's operations kept improving while its share price fell.</li><li><b>Your internal skills may be a product.</b> AWS grew from what Amazon had learned to run its own systems.</li></ol><h2>One action to try</h2><p>Write down three things your customers, employer or family will still want ten years from now. Ask whether your current work is getting better at any of them.</p><h2>Key idea</h2><p><b>Short-term noise is loud. Long-term value is quiet. Build for the second.</b></p><p class='disclaimer'>Educational content, not investment advice or a recommendation on any security.</p>`},
 {slug:`elon-musk-risk-and-failure`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-10-07`,read:7,
  sources:[`SpaceX: Falcon 1 flight history (spacex.com)`,`NASA press release on Commercial Resupply Services contracts awarded to SpaceX and Orbital Sciences, 23 December 2008`,`eBay press release on completion of the PayPal acquisition, October 2002`,`Compaq announcement of the acquisition of Zip2, February 1999`,`Tesla, Inc., Form S-1 registration statement, US SEC, January 2010`],
  title:`Elon Musk: what three failed rocket launches teach about risk`,
  lede:`Elon Musk's companies are famous, but the most useful part of his story is 2008, when SpaceX and Tesla were both close to failure. It shows the power of persistence, and the very real cost of extreme risk.`,
  body:`<p><i>This story is based on publicly documented company history. Musk is a controversial figure and his approach is not a model to copy wholesale. The lessons here are about risk, failure and learning.</i></p><h2>The early companies</h2><p>In 1995, Elon Musk and his brother Kimbal started Zip2, which provided online city guides and maps for newspapers. Compaq bought it in 1999. Musk then co-founded X.com, an online payments company, which merged with a rival called Confinity in 2000. The combined company became PayPal, and eBay acquired it in 2002 for about 1.5 billion dollars.</p><p>Musk could have stopped there. Instead, he put a large share of his money into two industries where most new entrants fail: rockets and electric cars.</p><h2>SpaceX: three failures in a row</h2><p>Musk founded SpaceX in 2002 with the goal of making space launches far cheaper. Its first rocket, Falcon 1, failed on its first launch in 2006. The second attempt in 2007 also failed. The third, in August 2008, failed as well.</p><p>By then, the company had money for roughly one more attempt. On 28 September 2008, the fourth Falcon 1 launch reached orbit, the first privately developed liquid-fuelled rocket to do so.</p><h2>Tesla: close to running out of money</h2><p>Tesla was founded in 2003 by Martin Eberhard and Marc Tarpenning. Musk led its early funding rounds, became chairman, and in October 2008 became CEO. The financial crisis hit as Tesla was struggling to produce its first car, the Roadster, and the company was close to running out of cash. A funding round closed in December 2008.</p><p>The same month, NASA awarded SpaceX a contract to carry cargo to the International Space Station, worth about 1.6 billion dollars. Both companies survived.</p><h2>What is easy to misunderstand</h2><p>This story is often told as "bet everything and you will win". That reading is dangerous. In 2008, both companies came close to failing at the same time. Musk has spoken publicly about how near they came. Many founders who take similar risks lose everything, and we rarely hear their stories. This is survivorship bias.</p><p>It is also worth separating the person from the lessons. Musk has been widely criticised over the years, including over public statements, management style and some business decisions. A success story is not a story of a flawless person.</p><h2>The principle</h2><p><b>Failure is information, but survival comes first. You can only learn from a failure you are still around after.</b></p><h2>The reasoning</h2><p>SpaceX treated each failed launch as data: what broke, why, and what to change. That is a sound principle for any project. The extreme part was the financial position: concentrated risk with little room left. For individuals, and especially families depending on one income, the sensible lesson is the learning habit, not the all-in bet.</p><h2>A Pakistan angle</h2><p>Many people in Pakistan put most of their savings into one plot, one business or one hot stock. When it works, it becomes a family success story. When it fails, it can set a family back for years. Musk's story shows how close even a very well-funded founder came to losing. Most of us do not have his margin for error.</p><h2>Three practical lessons</h2><ol><li><b>Treat every failure as a test result.</b> Write down what went wrong and what you will change next time.</li><li><b>Know how many attempts you can afford.</b> Before starting a project or investment, decide how much time and money you can lose and still be fine.</li><li><b>Hard problems take years.</b> SpaceX's first successful launch came six years after it was founded.</li></ol><h2>One action to try</h2><p>For a project or investment you are considering, write: "I can afford to try this ___ times, losing up to ___ PKR in total, without harming my family's essentials." If you cannot fill in the numbers, the plan is not ready.</p><h2>Key idea</h2><p><b>Persist with the learning, not with the risk you cannot afford.</b></p><p class='disclaimer'>Educational content, not financial or business advice.</p>`},
 {slug:`cz-binance-fast-growth-and-rules`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-10-07`,read:7,
  sources:[`US Department of Justice press release: Binance and CEO plead guilty to federal charges, 21 November 2023`,`US Department of Justice: sentencing of Changpeng Zhao, 30 April 2024`,`Binance: company history and launch announcement, July 2017`,`Pakistan Crypto Council announcement of Changpeng Zhao as adviser, April 2025`],
  title:`CZ and Binance: building the world's largest crypto exchange, and why rules matter`,
  lede:`Changpeng Zhao, known as CZ, built Binance into the world's largest cryptocurrency exchange by trading volume within months. He later pleaded guilty in the US over failures in its anti-money-laundering controls. Both halves of the story carry lessons.`,
  body:`<p><i>This story is based on publicly documented facts, including US court records. It is not an endorsement of any exchange or crypto asset. Crypto carries very high risk.</i></p><h2>The background</h2><p>Changpeng Zhao was born in China and grew up partly in Canada. He studied computer science and worked on trading software, including at Bloomberg Tradebook. In 2013 he became interested in bitcoin, joined Blockchain.info, and later worked as chief technology officer at the exchange OKCoin.</p><h2>The fast rise</h2><p>In July 2017, during a period of intense crypto excitement, Zhao launched Binance. It raised money by selling its own token, BNB. Binance focused on speed, a large choice of coins, low trading fees and a strong technical platform. Within months it became one of the largest crypto exchanges by trading volume, and it was widely described as the world's largest.</p><p>Its growth showed real strengths: a team that could build reliable trading systems quickly, close attention to what active traders wanted, and the willingness to expand globally at great speed.</p><h2>The problem</h2><p>Growing that fast across many countries created a serious weakness. In November 2023, Binance and Zhao pleaded guilty in the United States to charges related to failing to maintain an effective anti-money-laundering programme and violations of US sanctions law. Binance agreed to pay about 4.3 billion dollars, and Zhao stepped down as CEO. In April 2024 he was sentenced to four months in prison.</p><h2>What was misunderstood</h2><p>A common belief in fast-moving industries is that rules can be fixed later, once the business is big enough. The Binance case shows that compliance, knowing who your customers are and preventing money laundering, is part of the core product for any financial business. Ignoring it does not remove the risk; it delays and enlarges it.</p><h2>A Pakistan angle</h2><p>Crypto is popular among young Pakistanis, while the country's rules on digital assets are still developing. In 2025, Pakistan set up a Pakistan Crypto Council, which announced Zhao as an adviser. For ordinary users, the practical points are simple: rules are changing, exchanges differ greatly in safety and regulation, and you should understand the legal status in Pakistan before using any platform.</p><h2>The principle</h2><p><b>Speed builds a business. Trust and rules keep it alive.</b></p><h2>Three practical lessons</h2><ol><li><b>Building fast is a real skill.</b> Binance's growth came from strong technology and listening to users, both worth learning from.</li><li><b>Compliance is not paperwork; it is protection.</b> For any business that handles other people's money, rules on identity, record-keeping and fraud are part of the product.</li><li><b>As a user, judge platforms on safety, not only fees.</b> Who regulates it? Where is it based? What happens to your money if something goes wrong?</li></ol><h2>One action to try</h2><p>If you use any app that holds your money, whether a crypto exchange, wallet or broker, write down who regulates it, in which country, and how you would withdraw your money if the platform had problems. If you cannot answer, find out before adding more funds.</p><h2>Key idea</h2><p><b>Growth that ignores the rules is borrowed time.</b></p><p class='disclaimer'>Educational content only. Not financial advice or a recommendation to use any exchange or buy any crypto asset. Crypto prices are highly volatile and you can lose all of your money.</p>`},
 {slug:`jack-dorsey-twitter-square-block`,pillar:`Mind`,date:`2026-10-07`,read:7,
  sources:[`Twitter, Inc., Form S-1 registration statement, US SEC, October 2013`,`Square, Inc., Form S-1 registration statement, US SEC, October 2015`,`Block, Inc., press release announcing the name change from Square, 1 December 2021`,`Jack Dorsey, first public tweet, 21 March 2006`,`State Bank of Pakistan: Raast instant payment system (sbp.org.pk)`],
  title:`Jack Dorsey: Twitter, Square and the habit of fixing small frustrations`,
  lede:`Jack Dorsey co-founded two very different companies: a messaging platform and a payments business. Both started from a simple, specific problem. His path also includes being removed as CEO, which is part of the lesson.`,
  body:`<p><i>This story is based on publicly documented company history. Famous founders are rare exceptions; most startups fail. The value is in the thinking habits, not the outcome.</i></p><h2>The situation</h2><p>In the early 2000s, Jack Dorsey was a programmer who had worked on dispatch software, the systems that track taxis, couriers and emergency vehicles. He was interested in a simple idea: short status updates showing what people were doing, in real time, like a dispatch board for friends.</p><h2>Twitter: an idea from inside another company</h2><p>In 2006, Dorsey was working at Odeo, a podcasting company in San Francisco co-founded by Evan Williams and Noah Glass. Odeo's main business was under threat after Apple added podcasts to iTunes. The team looked for a new direction, and Dorsey's status-update idea was one of the projects. He sent the first public tweet on 21 March 2006: "just setting up my twttr".</p><p>Twitter was co-founded by Dorsey, Evan Williams, Biz Stone and Noah Glass. Dorsey became its first CEO.</p><h2>The setback</h2><p>In 2008, Dorsey was replaced as CEO. The company was growing fast, but the service frequently crashed and the board was unhappy with how it was being run. He remained chairman but was no longer in charge.</p><p>This is the part of the story that is often skipped. Being pushed out of a company you started is public and painful. What Dorsey did next is the more useful lesson.</p><h2>Square: a lost sale becomes a company</h2><p>In 2009, Dorsey's friend and former boss, Jim McKelvey, a glass artist, lost a sale of about 2,000 dollars because he could not accept a credit card. Small sellers at the time often found card machines expensive and complicated.</p><p>Dorsey and McKelvey founded Square. Its first well-known product was a small square card reader that plugged into a smartphone's headphone jack, letting anyone take card payments with a phone. The problem was specific, personal and shared by millions of small sellers.</p><h2>The results</h2><ul><li>Twitter listed its shares publicly in November 2013.</li><li>Square listed in November 2015. That year, Dorsey also returned as Twitter's CEO and ran both companies at the same time.</li><li>He stepped down as Twitter's CEO in November 2021. In December 2021, Square renamed itself Block, reflecting a wider range of financial products.</li></ul><h2>What is easy to misunderstand</h2><p>It is tempting to see a genius who had two great ideas. The documented history shows something more ordinary: one idea grew from his earlier work in dispatch software, and Twitter itself emerged from a company searching for a new direction. The second came from a friend's frustrating, everyday problem. Both took years, involved several co-founders, and included serious setbacks.</p><p>There is also a balanced point to make. Running two public companies at once was widely debated, and both companies faced criticism over the years. Success stories are not stories of perfect decisions.</p><h2>The principle</h2><p><b>Pay attention to small, repeated frustrations, your own and other people's. Some of them are businesses waiting to be built.</b></p><h2>A Pakistan angle</h2><p>The core problem Square solved, making it easy for small sellers to accept digital payments, is very real in Pakistan, where many shops and freelancers still rely on cash. Systems such as the State Bank of Pakistan's Raast instant payment system, mobile wallets and QR payments are changing that. Local frustrations around payments, delivery, documentation or trust remain large opportunities for people who understand them closely.</p><h2>Three practical lessons</h2><ol><li><b>Your past work is a source of ideas.</b> Dorsey's interest in real-time updates came from years of building dispatch software.</li><li><b>A specific story beats a general idea.</b> "A glass artist lost a 2,000 dollar sale" is clearer than "payments are hard".</li><li><b>A setback is not the end of a career.</b> Being removed as CEO in 2008 did not stop him from building Square a year later and returning to Twitter in 2015.</li></ol><h2>One action to try</h2><p>For one week, keep a note on your phone called "Frustrations". Every time you or someone near you says "why is this so difficult?", write it down with who was affected. At the end of the week, pick the one that affects the most people and ask: who is already trying to fix this, and what are they missing?</p><h2>Key idea</h2><p><b>Big companies often start as small irritations that someone refused to accept.</b></p>`},
 {slug:`careem-from-karachi-to-uber`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-10-07`,read:6,
  sources:[`Uber Technologies, press release on agreement to acquire Careem, 26 March 2019`,`Uber Technologies, announcement on completion of the Careem acquisition, January 2020`,`Careem company history, careem.com`],
  title:`Careem: how a regional car-booking idea became a 3.1 billion dollar deal`,
  lede:`Careem was co-founded by a Pakistani-American engineer, built much of its technology in Pakistan, and was bought by Uber. The lessons are less about luck and more about solving a local problem well.`,
  body:`<p><i>Success stories on this site focus on documented facts and practical lessons. Famous successes are the exceptions; most startups fail, and the lessons below are useful whether or not you ever build a company.</i></p><h2>The situation</h2><p>In 2012, Mudassir Sheikha and Magnus Olsson, two former management consultants, started Careem in Dubai. Abdulla Elyas joined as a co-founder. Their first product was simple: a way for companies to book reliable cars for their staff.</p><p>At the time, getting a dependable taxi in many Middle Eastern and South Asian cities was difficult. Global ride-hailing apps were focused on other markets.</p><h2>What they did differently</h2><ul><li><b>Started narrow.</b> Corporate bookings were a smaller, more predictable business than serving every rider from day one.</li><li><b>Built for local conditions.</b> Careem accepted cash payments in markets where card use was low, and adapted to addresses that are often descriptive rather than precise. These details matter in cities like Karachi and Lahore.</li><li><b>Built talent in Pakistan.</b> Careem developed a large technology and operations presence in Pakistan, and launched its ride service in Pakistani cities in 2016.</li><li><b>Expanded city by city</b> across the Middle East, North Africa and Pakistan.</li></ul><h2>Why it was hard</h2><p>Ride-hailing is a difficult business. It needs enough drivers to keep waiting times short, enough riders to keep drivers busy, local permissions in every city, and trust on both sides. Each new country brought different laws, payment habits and safety concerns. Careem also faced strong competition once Uber entered the same markets, and both companies spent heavily to win riders and drivers.</p><p>The company also built a reputation around its <b>captains</b>, as it called its drivers, and invested in training and support for them. Whether you build a company or work inside one, the people who deliver the service are part of the product.</p><h2>The result</h2><p>In March 2019, Uber announced an agreement to acquire Careem for 3.1 billion US dollars, and the deal was completed in January 2020. It was widely reported as one of the largest technology deals in the region at the time.</p><h2>What is easy to misunderstand</h2><p>Stories like this are often told as overnight success. The documented timeline shows about seven years between founding and the acquisition, with expansion into many difficult markets along the way. The idea itself, booking a car through an app, was not new. The execution for a specific region was the difference.</p><h2>The broader picture</h2><p>The Careem deal drew global attention to the region's technology talent, including engineers in Pakistan. It is worth remembering, though, that for every widely reported success there are many startups in Pakistan and elsewhere that close without headlines. Studying failures is just as useful as studying wins, even if it is less exciting.</p><h2>The principle</h2><p><b>A familiar idea, built carefully for a market that others overlook, can become a large business.</b></p><h2>Three practical lessons</h2><ol><li><b>Local knowledge is an advantage.</b> Understanding how people actually pay, travel and communicate in your city is something global companies often lack.</li><li><b>Start with a narrow customer.</b> A smaller, clearly defined first market lets you learn before you scale.</li><li><b>Plan for years, not months.</b> Building something significant usually takes a long time, with setbacks along the way.</li></ol><h2>One action to try</h2><p>Write down one problem you face every week in your city that existing apps or services handle badly. Then write who else has the same problem and how they solve it today.</p><h2>Key idea</h2><p><b>You do not need a new idea. You need a real problem, understood better than anyone else understands it.</b></p>`},
 {slug:`airbnb-air-mattresses-to-global`,pillar:`Mind`,date:`2026-10-07`,read:5,
  sources:[`Airbnb, Inc., Form S-1 registration statement, filed with the US SEC, November 2020`,`Airbnb newsroom, company history and founders`,`Y Combinator, company directory entry for Airbnb (Winter 2009 batch)`],
  title:`Airbnb: three air mattresses, cereal boxes and a lot of rejection`,
  lede:`Airbnb began with three air mattresses on a flat floor in San Francisco. Its early years show how persistence, small experiments and listening to users can turn an odd idea into a global company.`,
  body:`<p><i>This story is based on publicly documented company history. Famous successes are rare exceptions; the value here is in the habits, not the outcome.</i></p><h2>The situation</h2><p>In 2007, Brian Chesky and Joe Gebbia were struggling to pay rent in San Francisco. A large design conference was coming to the city and hotels were full. They put air mattresses on their floor, made a simple website, and offered guests a bed and breakfast. A few people paid.</p><p>With Nathan Blecharczyk, they turned the experiment into a company in 2008.</p><h2>The problem</h2><p>The early idea was hard to sell. Many investors were unconvinced that people would stay in strangers' homes. Growth was slow and the founders ran short of money.</p><p>To keep going, they designed and sold limited-edition cereal boxes themed on the 2008 US presidential election, "Obama O's" and "Cap'n McCain's", and used the money to keep the company alive.</p><h2>What changed</h2><ul><li><b>They got into Y Combinator</b>, a startup programme, in early 2009, which gave them funding and advice.</li><li><b>They met their users.</b> The founders travelled to meet hosts in New York, one of their key markets, and noticed that poor photos made listings unattractive. They helped hosts improve their listing photos, and bookings in that market improved.</li><li><b>They kept improving trust,</b> through reviews, profiles and secure payments, because trust was the core barrier to the idea.</li></ul><h2>What the early users taught them</h2><p>The founders learned that their first assumption, that the product was mainly for conferences when hotels were full, was too narrow. Travellers wanted cheaper, more local places to stay at any time, and hosts wanted extra income from space they already had. By listening, they found a much larger market than the one they started with.</p><p>They also did things that did not scale, such as personally visiting hosts. Early on, these small, manual efforts taught them more than any amount of planning.</p><h2>The result</h2><p>Airbnb grew into a global platform and listed its shares publicly in the United States in December 2020.</p><h2>What is easy to misunderstand</h2><p>It is tempting to read this as "never give up and you will succeed". That is survivorship bias: we hear the stories of those who persisted and won, not the far larger number who persisted and still failed. The more useful lesson is <b>how</b> they persisted: with small, cheap experiments and close attention to what users actually did.</p><h2>Three practical lessons</h2><ol><li><b>Test an idea cheaply first.</b> Three air mattresses and a basic website were enough to learn whether anyone would pay.</li><li><b>Go and see your users.</b> Problems like bad photos are invisible from a spreadsheet.</li><li><b>Find the core fear and solve it.</b> For Airbnb it was trust between strangers. Every business has one main barrier.</li></ol><h2>One action to try</h2><p>Take an idea you have been thinking about and design a one-week test that costs almost nothing: a WhatsApp catalogue, a simple form, or ten conversations with possible customers. Write down what you expect, then what actually happens.</p><h2>Key idea</h2><p><b>Persistence works best when it is paired with fast, cheap learning.</b></p>`},
 {slug:`fat-loss-myths`,pillar:`Body`,date:`2026-10-07`,read:6,
  sources:[`Vispute SS et al. (2011). The effect of abdominal exercise on abdominal fat. Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research`,`Ramírez-Campillo R et al. (2013). Regional fat changes induced by localized muscle endurance resistance training. Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research`,`NHS: Losing weight; and Sweating and body temperature guidance`,`World Health Organization (2020). Guidelines on physical activity and sedentary behaviour`],
  title:`Three common fat-loss myths, and what the evidence actually shows`,
  lede:`Gym advice, social media and family wisdom repeat ideas about fat loss that sound logical but do not match the evidence. Knowing the difference saves time, money and frustration.`,
  body:`<h2>Why these myths are common</h2><p>Fat loss is slow and hard to see day to day, so people look for shortcuts and visible signs of progress. Myths fill that gap: they offer a simple cause and a quick fix. These three are widely repeated in gyms, online and in conversation, and each has been studied.</p><h2>Myth 1: "Crunches will burn belly fat"</h2><p><b>Reality:</b> you cannot choose where your body loses fat by exercising that area. This idea is called spot reduction.</p><p><b>What the evidence shows:</b> in a controlled study (Vispute and colleagues, 2011), six weeks of abdominal exercises strengthened the muscles but did not significantly reduce belly fat compared with a group that did not do them. Another study (Ramírez-Campillo and colleagues, 2013) trained only one leg for 12 weeks; fat loss came from the whole body, including the upper body, rather than mainly from the trained leg.</p><p><b>What to do:</b> core exercises are still useful for strength and posture. For fat loss, focus on overall habits: regular activity, strength training for the whole body, sleep, and an eating pattern you can sustain.</p><h2>Myth 2: "The more I sweat, the more fat I burn"</h2><p><b>Reality:</b> sweat is how the body cools itself. It reflects heat, humidity, clothing and individual differences, not fat burned.</p><p><b>What the evidence shows:</b> the weight lost through sweating is water, and it returns when you drink. Exercising in plastic suits or very hot rooms to sweat more increases the risk of dehydration and heat illness, which is especially relevant in Pakistan's summer heat.</p><p><b>Practical implication:</b> judge a workout by consistency and gradual progress, not sweat. Train in cooler hours, wear breathable clothing and replace fluids.</p><h2>Myth 3: "Eating after 8 pm makes you gain fat"</h2><p><b>Reality:</b> there is no clock time at which food automatically turns into fat. Total energy intake over days and weeks matters most.</p><p><b>What the evidence shows:</b> controlled feeding studies generally find that weight change depends mainly on total intake. Some research suggests late eating can affect appetite, sleep and blood sugar for some people, so timing is not completely irrelevant, but the effect is smaller than the myth suggests. Late-night eating is also often linked with extra snacks rather than the meal itself.</p><p><b>Practical implication:</b> if late dinners are part of your family routine, which is common in many Pakistani households, you do not need to skip them. Pay attention to portions, fried and sugary extras, and leave some time between eating and sleep if late meals disturb your rest.</p><h2>What is genuinely known</h2><ul><li>Fat loss happens across the whole body, not where you exercise.</li><li>Sweat is mainly about temperature control.</li><li>Over time, total energy intake and activity are central to body weight.</li><li>Regular activity, including muscle-strengthening on two or more days a week, benefits health at any body size, as reflected in WHO guidelines.</li></ul><h2>What remains uncertain</h2><ul><li>How much meal timing matters for different people, and through which mechanisms.</li><li>Why people respond so differently to the same diet or training plan; genetics, sleep, stress and medication all play a part.</li><li>Which approach is best for any one individual over the long term.</li></ul><h2>Three practical rules</h2><ol><li><b>Train the whole body, not just the area you want to change.</b></li><li><b>Measure progress over weeks,</b> using a few markers such as energy, strength, waist measurement or how clothes fit, rather than daily sweat or weight swings.</li><li><b>Choose habits you can keep for a year,</b> not plans that require extreme effort for a month.</li></ol><p class='disclaimer'>General health information, not medical advice. People with medical conditions, or who are pregnant, should speak to a doctor before changing diet or exercise. Weight and body shape are personal; this article is about understanding evidence, not judging bodies.</p>`},
 {slug:`panic-selling-after-a-market-fall`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-10-06`,read:5,
  sources:[`Kahneman D, Tversky A (1979). Prospect theory: an analysis of decision under risk. Econometrica`,`Morningstar, Mind the Gap annual studies of investor returns versus fund returns`,`Barber BM, Odean T (2000). Trading is hazardous to your wealth. Journal of Finance`],
  title:`He sold everything after a 15% fall. What went wrong?`,
  lede:`A hypothetical but familiar story: an investor panics during a sharp PSX drop, sells everything, and then watches from the sidelines. The mistake was made long before the sale.`,
  body:`<p><i>This is a hypothetical example, built from a common pattern. The names and numbers are illustrative.</i></p><h2>The situation</h2><p>Hamza, 32, works in Islamabad. Two years ago he started investing 20,000 PKR a month in a handful of PSX shares. His portfolio grew to about 600,000 PKR, and he checked it every evening.</p><p>Then the market fell sharply over three weeks. News channels talked about political uncertainty. His WhatsApp groups were full of warnings. His portfolio dropped to about 510,000 PKR, a fall of 15%.</p><h2>The decision</h2><p>One evening, after watching his balance fall again, Hamza sold everything. "I will buy back when things settle," he told himself. The relief was immediate.</p><p>Over the following months the market recovered part of the fall. Hamza did not buy back. Every rise felt like it might be a trap, and every dip felt like the start of another crash. A year later his money was still sitting in his bank account.</p><h2>What was misunderstood</h2><p>Hamza thought his problem was the market fall. His real problem was that <b>he never decided in advance how he would respond to one</b>.</p><ul><li>He treated money he might need in the near future and long-term money the same way.</li><li>He had no written rule for what a fall should trigger.</li><li>He confused a temporary price drop with a permanent loss. Selling made the loss permanent.</li><li>He thought selling was one decision. It was two: when to get out, and when to get back in. The second is usually harder.</li></ul><h2>The principle</h2><p><b>Decide your response to volatility before it happens, while you are calm.</b></p><h2>The reasoning behind it</h2><p><b>Established:</b> stock markets, including the PSX, regularly go through sharp falls. Prices of individual shares can fall much further, and some never recover. Volatility is a normal feature of owning shares, not a sign that something unusual is happening.</p><p><b>Established:</b> research in behavioural finance has found that many individual investors earn lower returns than the funds or markets they invest in, partly because they buy after rises and sell after falls. Studies of fund flows in several countries show this "behaviour gap", though its size varies by study and period.</p><p><b>Established:</b> people tend to feel losses more strongly than equal gains. Psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky called this loss aversion. A falling balance checked every day creates many small painful moments, which increases the urge to act.</p><p><b>Interpretation:</b> if your plan depends on staying calm during a fall, the plan should make calm easier. Rules written in advance, and less frequent checking, are practical ways to do that.</p><h2>A better approach</h2><ol><li><b>Separate the money.</b> Money needed within about three years, and the emergency fund, should not be in shares. Only long-term money belongs there.</li><li><b>Write a volatility rule.</b> For example: "A market fall of up to 30% does not change my plan. I will review my holdings, not sell them by default." The number is a personal choice, not a recommendation.</li><li><b>Separate the business from the price.</b> In a fall, ask whether anything changed in the companies you own: profits, debt, management. If the business is still sound, the price drop alone is not a reason to sell. If the business has truly worsened, that is a separate, valid reason.</li><li><b>Check less often.</b> Reviewing monthly or quarterly instead of daily reduces emotional pressure without making you less informed about what matters.</li><li><b>Keep investing on schedule</b> if your situation has not changed. Regular monthly investing means you buy more shares when prices are lower.</li></ol><h2>Three practical lessons</h2><ol><li><b>A fall you planned for is uncomfortable. A fall you did not plan for is dangerous.</b></li><li><b>Selling is easy. Getting back in is the hard part.</b> If you exit, you need a written re-entry rule too.</li><li><b>Your time horizon is your protection.</b> Money you will not need for many years can afford to wait; money you need soon cannot.</li></ol><h2>One action to try</h2><p>Write three lines and keep them with your investment records: <b>"My investing money is for ____ (goal), needed in ____ years. If the market falls by ____%, I will ____. I review my portfolio every ____."</b></p><h2>Principle</h2><p><b>Make your decisions on calm days, so you do not have to make them on frightening ones.</b></p><p class='disclaimer'>Educational content only. Not financial advice or a recommendation to buy, hold or sell any security. Share prices can fall and may not recover. Past market recoveries do not guarantee future ones.</p>`},
 {slug:`pay-yourself-first-system`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-10-06`,read:6,
  sources:[`Thaler RH, Benartzi S (2004). Save More Tomorrow. Journal of Political Economy`,`Madrian BC, Shea DF (2001). The power of suggestion: inertia in 401(k) participation and savings behavior. Quarterly Journal of Economics`],
  title:`A simple monthly money system: four rules that run on autopilot`,
  lede:`Most budgets fail because they rely on willpower at the end of the month. This system moves the important decisions to the start of the month and makes them automatic.`,
  body:`<h2>The problem with the usual approach</h2><p>The usual approach is: get paid, spend through the month, and save whatever is left. In practice, very little is left. Bills arrive, family needs come up, a wedding season begins, prices rise. Saving becomes the leftover, and leftovers rarely appear.</p><p>Detailed spreadsheets with thirty categories have the opposite problem. They take too much effort, so most people stop updating them within weeks.</p><h2>The principle behind the framework</h2><p><b>Make the important decisions once, at the start of the month, and let the system carry them out.</b></p><p>There is solid evidence that defaults and automation change financial behaviour. Research by Richard Thaler and Shlomo Benartzi on automatic savings plans, and studies of automatic enrolment in pension schemes, found that people save far more when saving happens by default rather than by repeated choice. This framework applies that idea to everyday money. The framework itself is a practical method, not a scientifically tested programme.</p><h2>The four rules</h2><ol><li><b>Save first, on payday.</b> A fixed amount or percentage moves to a separate account the day your salary arrives.</li><li><b>Emergency fund before investing.</b> Savings first build a cushion of three to six months of essential costs. Only after that does money flow to long-term investing.</li><li><b>Three spending buckets.</b> Fixed costs (rent, bills, fees, loan payments), flexible living (food, transport, family), and personal spending. Each gets a monthly limit.</li><li><b>No new expensive debt for wants.</b> Credit cards and consumer loans are not used for things you could wait for.</li></ol><h2>Why each rule exists</h2><ul><li><b>Rule 1</b> removes the end-of-month willpower problem. What you never see in your spending account is harder to spend.</li><li><b>Rule 2</b> protects the investing plan. Without a cushion, any shock forces you to sell investments, often at a bad time.</li><li><b>Rule 3</b> gives enough structure to notice problems without needing to track every rupee. Three buckets are easy to maintain.</li><li><b>Rule 4</b> stops the system from leaking. High-interest debt can cost more than your investments are likely to earn.</li></ul><h2>How the rules interact</h2><p>Rule 1 creates the flow of savings. Rule 2 decides where that flow goes first. Rule 3 makes sure the remaining money covers your life. Rule 4 prevents a hidden drain that would undo the other three. If one bucket keeps running out, the fix is to adjust the limits or the saving amount, not to borrow.</p><h2>An example (hypothetical numbers)</h2><p>Sana earns 150,000 PKR a month after tax in Lahore.</p><div class='tablewrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Bucket</th><th>Monthly amount</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label='Bucket'>Saving (moved on payday)</td><td data-label='Monthly amount'>22,500 PKR (15%)</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Bucket'>Fixed costs</td><td data-label='Monthly amount'>60,000 PKR</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Bucket'>Flexible living</td><td data-label='Monthly amount'>50,000 PKR</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Bucket'>Personal spending</td><td data-label='Monthly amount'>17,500 PKR</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Her essential costs are about 110,000 PKR a month, so her emergency fund target is roughly 330,000 to 660,000 PKR. At 22,500 a month, reaching the lower target takes about 15 months. After that, the same transfer goes to her long-term investments.</p><h2>What happens when one rule is ignored</h2><ul><li><b>Skip Rule 1:</b> saving becomes occasional, and the emergency fund never fills.</li><li><b>Skip Rule 2:</b> a medical bill arrives while all savings are in shares during a market fall; she sells at a loss.</li><li><b>Skip Rule 3:</b> spending creeps up unnoticed until the saving transfer feels impossible.</li><li><b>Skip Rule 4:</b> a phone bought on a credit card at high interest quietly eats the savings for months.</li></ul><h2>A simple implementation process</h2><ol><li>Add up last month's essential costs from bank statements.</li><li>Choose a saving amount you can sustain. Starting at 5% is fine; increase it with each raise.</li><li>Open a separate savings account and set an automatic transfer for payday.</li><li>Set limits for the three buckets.</li><li>Write Rule 4 somewhere visible.</li></ol><h2>Weekly review checklist (10 minutes)</h2><ul><li>Did the saving transfer happen?</li><li>How much is left in flexible living and personal spending?</li><li>Any unplanned expense? Was it a true emergency?</li><li>Any new debt?</li><li>One adjustment for next week.</li></ul><h2>Final principle</h2><p><b>Do not rely on discipline every day of the month. Use it once, on payday, to build a system that does the rest.</b></p><p class='disclaimer'>Educational content only, with hypothetical numbers. Not personal financial advice.</p>`},
 {slug:`fibre-the-missing-nutrient`,pillar:`Body`,date:`2026-10-06`,read:6,
  sources:[`World Health Organization (2023). Carbohydrate intake for adults and children: WHO guideline`,`NHS: How to get more fibre into your diet`,`Reynolds A et al. (2019). Carbohydrate quality and human health: a series of systematic reviews and meta-analyses. The Lancet`],
  title:`Fibre: the nutrient most desi plates are missing`,
  lede:`Fibre gets less attention than protein, but it supports digestion, steady energy and long-term heart health. Most people can get enough from ordinary food they already know.`,
  body:`<h2>Why this matters</h2><p>Many modern diets in Pakistan lean on white flour, white rice, fried snacks and sugary drinks. These foods are filling in the moment but low in fibre. The result is often constipation, energy crashes after meals, and hunger that returns quickly. Over years, low-fibre eating is linked with higher risk of several chronic diseases.</p><h2>What the science says</h2><ul><li>The <b>World Health Organization</b> (2023 guideline on carbohydrate intake) recommends that adults eat at least 25 grams of naturally occurring dietary fibre per day.</li><li>The UK's <b>NHS</b> recommends about 30 grams a day for adults.</li><li>A large review of studies commissioned by the WHO and published in <i>The Lancet</i> (Reynolds and colleagues, 2019) found that people eating more fibre had lower rates of heart disease, stroke, type 2 diabetes and bowel cancer than those eating the least. These are observational findings, so they show strong associations rather than proof for any one person.</li></ul><h2>The basic mechanism</h2><ol><li><b>Fibre is the part of plant food your body cannot fully digest.</b> It passes through the gut largely intact.</li><li><b>Soluble fibre</b> (in oats, daal, chana, apples) forms a gel that slows digestion. This can help steady blood sugar after meals and lower LDL cholesterol.</li><li><b>Insoluble fibre</b> (in whole wheat, bran, vegetable skins) adds bulk and helps food move through the bowel.</li><li><b>Gut bacteria feed on some fibres,</b> producing compounds that support the gut lining. This is an active research area, and many details are still being studied.</li><li><b>Fibre adds fullness</b> without many calories, which can make portions easier to manage.</li></ol><h2>What a normal person needs to know</h2><p>Most adults in many countries eat less fibre than recommended, and refined staples are a common reason. In Pakistan, maida-based naan, white rice and packaged snacks are everyday foods, while traditional staples such as daal, chana, sabzi and chakki atta are naturally rich in fibre. The goal is not to remove favourite foods, but to tilt the balance back toward the traditional ones.</p><p>You do not need supplements or special products for most people. Aim for 25 to 30 grams a day from whole foods, increase slowly, and drink enough water as you do.</p><h2>Practical implementation</h2><ol><li>Swap some white flour for whole wheat (chakki) atta.</li><li>Add a bowl of daal or chana to at least one meal a day.</li><li>Eat fruit whole instead of as juice.</li><li>Include a vegetable sabzi or salad with lunch and dinner.</li><li>Increase by a few grams every few days, not all at once.</li></ol><h2>Desi food examples (approximate fibre)</h2><div class='tablewrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Food</th><th>Serving</th><th>Fibre</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label='Food'>Daal (cooked lentils)</td><td data-label='Serving'>1 cup</td><td data-label='Fibre'>about 13 to 15 g</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Food'>Chana (cooked chickpeas)</td><td data-label='Serving'>1 cup</td><td data-label='Fibre'>about 10 to 12 g</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Food'>Whole wheat roti</td><td data-label='Serving'>1 medium</td><td data-label='Fibre'>about 2 to 3 g</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Food'>Oats (dry)</td><td data-label='Serving'>40 g</td><td data-label='Fibre'>about 4 g</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Food'>Guava (amrood)</td><td data-label='Serving'>1 medium</td><td data-label='Fibre'>about 3 g</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Food'>Apple with skin</td><td data-label='Serving'>1 medium</td><td data-label='Fibre'>about 4 g</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Food'>Mixed vegetable sabzi</td><td data-label='Serving'>1 cup</td><td data-label='Fibre'>about 3 to 5 g</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A day with two whole wheat rotis, one cup of daal, one guava, one apple and a cup of sabzi gives roughly 30 grams.</p><h2>Common myths and mistakes</h2><ul><li><b>"Brown bread is always high-fibre."</b> Some brown breads are coloured white bread. Check the label for whole wheat as the first ingredient.</li><li><b>"Juice counts as fruit."</b> Juicing removes most of the fibre.</li><li><b>Increasing too fast</b> can cause bloating and gas. Go gradually.</li><li><b>Forgetting water.</b> More fibre without enough fluid can worsen constipation.</li></ul><h2>When this advice may not apply</h2><p>People with inflammatory bowel disease during a flare, some bowel conditions, recent bowel surgery, or those told to follow a low-fibre or low-residue diet should follow their doctor's or dietitian's guidance. People with kidney disease may have limits on some high-fibre foods because of potassium or phosphorus. Children's needs differ from adults'. This article is general health information, not medical advice.</p><h2>Simple checklist</h2><ul><li>Whole wheat atta at home</li><li>Daal or chana once a day</li><li>Two whole fruits a day</li><li>Vegetables at two meals</li><li>Water through the day</li><li>Increase gradually</li></ul><h2>Key takeaway</h2><p><b>You do not need a new diet for more fibre. You need more of the daal, sabzi, fruit and whole wheat your kitchen already knows.</b></p>`},
 {slug:`procrastination-is-a-mood-problem`,pillar:`Mind`,date:`2026-10-05`,read:6,
  sources:[`Steel P (2007). The nature of procrastination: a meta-analytic and theoretical review. Psychological Bulletin`,`Sirois F, Pychyl T (2013). Procrastination and the priority of short-term mood regulation. Social and Personality Psychology Compass`,`Wohl MJA, Pychyl TA, Bennett SH (2010). I forgive myself, now I can study. Personality and Individual Differences`,`Gollwitzer PM, Sheeran P (2006). Implementation intentions and goal achievement: a meta-analysis. Advances in Experimental Social Psychology`],
  title:`Procrastination: why we delay the things that matter`,
  lede:`Most procrastination is not laziness or poor time management. It is often a way of avoiding an unpleasant feeling. Seeing it that way makes it easier to change.`,
  body:`<h2>A familiar evening</h2><p>You come home planning to finish a report, study for an exam or open the spreadsheet of your monthly expenses. You make tea first. Then you check your phone for "two minutes". Forty minutes later you are watching cricket highlights, feeling guilty, and telling yourself you will start properly tomorrow morning.</p><p>Nothing about the task changed. It is still important, and you still intend to do it. Yet something pushed you away from it.</p><h2>What is happening psychologically</h2><p>Researchers usually define procrastination as <b>voluntarily delaying an intended action despite expecting to be worse off for the delay</b>. The key words are "intended" and "worse off". Resting, or choosing a different priority, is not procrastination. Delaying something you want to do, while knowing it will cost you, is.</p><p>A growing body of research, associated with psychologists such as Tim Pychyl and Fuschia Sirois, describes procrastination largely as a problem of <b>short-term mood repair</b>. The task triggers a feeling: boredom, confusion, fear of doing it badly, or resentment. Avoiding the task brings quick relief. The relief is real, but it is borrowed against later stress.</p><h2>Why behaviour tends to work this way</h2><ul><li><b>Present rewards feel larger than future ones.</b> This tendency, often called present bias or temporal discounting, is well documented in behavioural economics and psychology. Relief now outweighs a benefit next week.</li><li><b>Avoidance is reinforced.</b> Each time avoiding a task reduces discomfort, the brain learns that avoidance "works", which makes it more likely next time. This is a basic learning principle, not a personal flaw.</li><li><b>Vague tasks feel heavier.</b> "Work on my business plan" offers no clear first step, so the mind fills the gap with doubt.</li></ul><p>A large meta-analysis by Piers Steel (2007) found that procrastination is more common when tasks are unpleasant, when rewards are distant, and in people who are more impulsive or easily distracted. These are tendencies across many people, not fixed labels for any one person.</p><h2>The pattern people fall into</h2><p>Delay, relief, guilt, then a rush near the deadline. Because work done under pressure sometimes turns out fine, people conclude that they "work best under pressure". The cost of the stress, the lower quality, and the tasks that never got done at all are easy to forget.</p><h2>What makes it worse</h2><ul><li><b>Harsh self-criticism.</b> Studies suggest that people who forgave themselves for procrastinating on one exam procrastinated less before the next one. Self-blame adds another unpleasant feeling to the task.</li><li><b>Perfectionism and fear of judgement.</b> If the work must be excellent, starting becomes risky.</li><li><b>Constant easy escapes.</b> A phone within reach makes the relief available in one second.</li><li><b>Tiredness and poor sleep,</b> which reduce the capacity to tolerate discomfort.</li></ul><h2>How to recognise it in yourself</h2><p>Notice the moment just before you switch away. Ask: <b>"What am I feeling about this task right now?"</b> Common answers are bored, unsure where to start, worried it will not be good enough, or annoyed that it is needed at all. Naming the feeling turns a vague urge into something you can work with.</p><p>Other signs: you clean, organise or "research" instead of doing the core task; you keep rewriting the to-do list; the same item has moved from day to day for weeks.</p><h2>A practical intervention: shrink the start, not the goal</h2><p>Keep the goal, but make the first step small and concrete enough that the unpleasant feeling barely shows up.</p><ul><li>Not "write the report", but "open the file and write three rough bullet points".</li><li>Not "sort out my finances", but "list last month's five biggest expenses".</li><li>Not "study chapter four", but "read the first two pages with the phone in another room".</li></ul><p>There is good evidence for a related technique called <b>implementation intentions</b>: plans written in the form "When situation X happens, I will do Y". Studies by Peter Gollwitzer and colleagues found they increase follow-through on intended goals across many settings.</p><h2>A simple exercise: the ten-minute contract</h2><ol><li>Choose one task you have delayed for at least a week.</li><li>Write: "Tomorrow at ____ (time), at ____ (place), I will work on ____ for ten minutes."</li><li>Put the phone out of reach before the timer starts.</li><li>When ten minutes end, you may stop with no guilt. If you continue, that is a bonus.</li><li>Afterwards, write one line: what feeling came up, and how strong it was from 1 to 10.</li></ol><p>Starting is usually the hardest part. Many people find the feeling eases once they are a few minutes in, although this varies.</p><h2>How to measure whether it is working</h2><p>Track behaviour, not mood alone. For two weeks, record:</p><ul><li>How many planned starts actually happened.</li><li>How long, on average, an important task waits before you begin.</li><li>Your discomfort rating before starting, from 1 to 10.</li></ul><p>Progress looks like more starts, shorter waiting times and slightly lower discomfort. Perfect days are not the target. A trend is.</p><div class='callout'><b>When to seek help.</b> If avoidance is affecting your work, studies or relationships for a long time, or comes with low mood or strong anxiety, consider speaking to a qualified mental-health professional. Persistent procrastination can have many causes, and some deserve proper support.</div><h2>Final principle</h2><p><b>You do not need to feel ready. You need a start small enough that the feeling cannot stop you.</b></p>`},
 {slug:`sleep-debt-explained`,pillar:`Body`,date:`2026-10-05`,read:5,
  sources:[`Watson NF et al. (2015). Recommended amount of sleep for a healthy adult: consensus statement of the AASM and Sleep Research Society. Sleep`,`Van Dongen HPA et al. (2003). The cumulative cost of additional wakefulness. Sleep`,`Depner CM et al. (2019). Ad libitum weekend recovery sleep fails to prevent metabolic dysregulation during a repeating pattern of insufficient sleep. Current Biology`],
  title:`Sleep debt: what it is and why weekends cannot fully repay it`,
  lede:`Losing an hour of sleep a night feels harmless. Over a week it adds up, and it quietly affects your focus, mood, appetite and decisions.`,
  body:`<h2>Why it matters</h2><p>Late cricket matches, Ramadan sehri, long commutes, exams, night shifts and phone screens: in Pakistan, as everywhere, sleep is often the first thing people cut. One short night rarely feels serious. But most people cut sleep a little at a time, night after night, and the effects build quietly.</p><h2>What sleep debt is</h2><p><b>Sleep debt</b> is the difference between the sleep your body needs and the sleep you actually get, added up over days. If you need about seven and a half hours and sleep six, you build a debt of roughly one and a half hours each night, or about ten hours over a week.</p><p>Health bodies such as the American Academy of Sleep Medicine recommend that most adults get at least seven hours a night on a regular basis. Individual needs vary, and teenagers usually need more.</p><h2>Why it matters in daily life</h2><ul><li><b>Attention and reaction time</b> fall. Studies that limited people to about six hours a night for two weeks found their performance on attention tests dropped steadily, close to the level seen after a full night without sleep.</li><li><b>People underestimate how impaired they are.</b> In the same research, participants reported feeling only slightly sleepy while their performance kept getting worse.</li><li><b>Mood and patience</b> drop, making conflicts at home and work more likely.</li><li><b>Appetite</b> tends to rise, especially for sugary and high-fat food, which makes weight management harder.</li><li><b>Risk on the road</b> rises. Drowsy driving is a recognised cause of accidents.</li></ul><p>Over the longer term, research links regularly short sleep with higher risk of weight gain, type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure and heart disease. These studies show associations, and many factors are involved, but the pattern is consistent enough that health bodies treat sleep as a basic pillar of health alongside food and activity.</p><h2>How it works</h2><ol><li><b>Sleep pressure builds while you are awake.</b> A chemical signal, adenosine, accumulates in the brain and makes you feel sleepy. Sleep clears it.</li><li><b>Your body clock sets the timing.</b> The circadian rhythm, guided mainly by light, decides when you feel alert and when you feel tired.</li><li><b>Short sleep leaves pressure uncleared.</b> You start the day already behind.</li><li><b>Caffeine masks it.</b> Tea and coffee block the sleepy signal for a few hours, but they do not remove the debt.</li><li><b>Naps help only partly.</b> A short nap of 20 to 30 minutes in the early afternoon can improve alertness. Long or late naps can make it harder to fall asleep at night, which keeps the cycle going.</li></ol><p>Shift workers and people whose schedules change, such as during Ramadan, face an extra challenge because sleep happens at times the body clock is not expecting. Protecting a consistent block of sleep, using a dark, quiet room, and planning short naps can reduce, though not remove, the effect.</p><h2>Common misunderstanding: "I will catch up at the weekend"</h2><p>Sleeping longer on Friday and Saturday helps you feel better, and some recovery does happen. However, studies suggest that weekend catch-up sleep does not fully reverse the effects of a week of short sleep on attention and metabolism. Large swings in sleep timing between weekdays and weekends can also shift your body clock, making Sunday night and Monday morning harder. This is sometimes called social jet lag.</p><p>The more reliable fix is a regular schedule with enough sleep on most nights.</p><h2>A practical example</h2><p>Bilal, a 28-year-old in Lahore, wakes at 6:30 for work and usually sleeps around 12:30 after scrolling his phone. That is six hours. By Thursday he relies on three cups of chai, feels short-tempered in the afternoon and snacks more at night. At the weekend he sleeps until 11, then cannot fall asleep before 2 on Sunday night, and Monday starts badly. Moving his bedtime 30 minutes earlier each week, and keeping a similar wake time on weekends, would reduce both his debt and the Monday reset.</p><h2>What to do</h2><ol><li><b>Fix your wake-up time first,</b> including weekends, within about an hour.</li><li><b>Move bedtime earlier in small steps,</b> 15 to 30 minutes at a time.</li><li><b>Get daylight in the morning</b> and keep evening light dim.</li><li><b>Set a caffeine cut-off,</b> for example no tea or coffee after mid-afternoon.</li><li><b>Keep the phone out of reach in bed,</b> or use an alarm clock instead.</li></ol><div class='callout'><b>See a doctor if</b> you snore loudly, stop breathing during sleep, feel very sleepy despite enough time in bed, or have long-term trouble falling or staying asleep. These can be signs of conditions that need medical assessment.</div><h2>Key takeaway</h2><p><b>Sleep is not a bank you can raid all week and refill on Sunday. Regular, sufficient sleep is the repayment plan.</b></p>`},
 {slug:`cardio-vs-strength-training`,pillar:`Body`,date:`2026-10-05`,read:7,
  sources:[`World Health Organization (2020). Guidelines on physical activity and sedentary behaviour`,`Momma H et al. (2022). Muscle-strengthening activities are associated with lower risk and mortality in major non-communicable diseases: a systematic review and meta-analysis. British Journal of Sports Medicine`],
  title:`Cardio vs strength training: what each does and who it suits`,
  lede:`Cardio and strength training are often presented as rivals. They solve different problems. Here is what the evidence says about each, and how to think about your own choice.`,
  body:`<h2>What each option is</h2><p><b>Cardio</b> (aerobic exercise) is continuous movement that raises your heart rate and breathing for a sustained period: brisk walking, jogging, cycling, swimming or skipping rope.</p><p><b>Strength training</b> (resistance training) means working your muscles against a load: body weight, dumbbells, machines, resistance bands or even filled water bottles. Push-ups, squats, rows and lunges are common examples.</p><h2>What problem each solves</h2><p>Cardio mainly improves the heart, lungs and circulation, and builds endurance for everyday activity. Strength training mainly builds and preserves muscle, strength and bone, which matter for posture, joints and staying independent with age.</p><h2>How each works</h2><ul><li><b>Cardio</b> repeatedly asks the heart to pump more blood and the muscles to use more oxygen. Over weeks, the heart pumps more efficiently and resting heart rate often falls.</li><li><b>Strength training</b> creates a challenge the muscle is not used to. With enough protein and rest, the body adapts by making muscle fibres stronger and, over time, larger. Bones also respond to load.</li></ul><h2>The evidence</h2><p><b>Established:</b> The World Health Organization recommends adults do 150 to 300 minutes of moderate aerobic activity per week (or 75 to 150 minutes of vigorous activity), and muscle-strengthening activities on two or more days per week. In other words, the guidance includes both.</p><p><b>Established:</b> Regular physical activity is linked with lower risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes, some cancers and depression. Large studies also link muscle-strengthening activity with lower risk of early death, independent of aerobic activity.</p><p><b>Interpretation:</b> Because each type covers gaps the other leaves, most people likely benefit from some of both. How to split your time depends on goals, health and preferences.</p><h2>Advantages</h2><p><b>Cardio:</b> easy to start, often free (walking), good for heart and lung fitness, helps with stamina, can be social, and moderate cardio is generally low-skill.</p><p><b>Strength training:</b> builds and keeps muscle, supports bone density, improves strength for daily tasks such as lifting children or shopping bags, may support blood-sugar control, and sessions can be short.</p><h2>Limitations</h2><p><b>Cardio:</b> on its own, does little to maintain muscle mass with age; high-impact forms like running can aggravate some joints; outdoor sessions are hard in extreme summer heat and poor air quality, both real issues in many Pakistani cities.</p><p><b>Strength training:</b> needs some technique to do safely; progress requires gradually increasing the challenge; equipment or a gym may cost money; beginners can feel intimidated.</p><h2>Risk, cost and time</h2><div class='tablewrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Cardio</th><th>Strength training</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label='Factor'>Minimum time (guideline)</td><td data-label='Cardio'>150 min/week moderate</td><td data-label='Strength training'>2 sessions/week</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Factor'>Cost to start</td><td data-label='Cardio'>Can be zero (walking)</td><td data-label='Strength training'>Zero with body weight; gym fees vary widely</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Factor'>Skill needed</td><td data-label='Cardio'>Low for walking, higher for swimming</td><td data-label='Strength training'>Moderate: technique matters</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Factor'>Main injury risk</td><td data-label='Cardio'>Overuse (doing too much too soon)</td><td data-label='Strength training'>Poor form or lifting too heavy too early</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Factor'>Main benefit</td><td data-label='Cardio'>Heart, lungs, endurance</td><td data-label='Strength training'>Muscle, strength, bone</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Factor'>Pakistan challenge</td><td data-label='Cardio'>Summer heat, smog, safe walking space</td><td data-label='Strength training'>Access to equipment, coaching quality</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>Who each may suit</h2><p><b>More cardio may suit</b> someone who is currently inactive and needs an easy entry point, someone focused on stamina, or someone who enjoys outdoor routines like a morning walk after Fajr.</p><p><b>More strength may suit</b> someone who already walks regularly, people over 40 concerned about losing muscle, desk workers with weak backs and shoulders, and anyone who wants to feel physically capable for daily tasks.</p><p><b>A combined example:</b> Ayesha, 35, works at a desk in Karachi and walks very little. A realistic starting week might be three 30-minute brisk walks in the early morning or in a mall during summer, plus two 20-minute home strength sessions with squats to a chair, wall push-ups, rows using a backpack, and glute bridges. That already meets most of the guideline with no gym fee. After a month, she could lengthen the walks or add weight to the backpack.</p><p><b>Interpretation:</b> for many beginners, the question is less "cardio or strength?" and more "how do I fit in a small amount of both that I can keep up?"</p><h2>When one should not be used, or needs caution</h2><ul><li>Avoid hard outdoor cardio during heatwaves or very poor air quality days; move it indoors or to early morning.</li><li>Avoid heavy lifting with acute back pain, recent surgery, or uncontrolled high blood pressure without medical advice.</li><li>People with heart disease, diabetes complications, pregnancy or joint conditions should ask a doctor which forms and intensities are safe.</li><li>Any exercise that causes chest pain, fainting or severe breathlessness should stop immediately, followed by medical assessment.</li></ul><h2>Questions to ask before choosing</h2><ol><li>What is my main goal right now: stamina, strength, weight management or general health?</li><li>What can I realistically do three times a week, given work, family and weather?</li><li>Do I have any medical condition that needs a doctor's input first?</li><li>Do I know how to do the basic movements safely, or do I need guidance?</li><li>Which option do I actually enjoy enough to keep doing?</li></ol><h2>A balanced conclusion</h2><p>Cardio and strength training are not competitors; they target different parts of health. The evidence-based guidelines include both. For an individual, the "right" balance depends on starting point, goals, health, time and enjoyment. A routine you keep for years is worth more than an ideal plan you abandon in a month.</p><p>If you are unsure where to start, a common middle path is to begin with walking for consistency, add two short strength sessions, and adjust the balance every few months based on how you feel, what your goals are, and any advice from a doctor or qualified trainer.</p><p class='disclaimer'>General education, not personal medical advice. Check with a doctor before starting a new exercise programme if you have a health condition.</p>`},
 {slug:`dividend-yield-explained`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-10-05`,read:7,
  sources:[`Pakistan Stock Exchange: company announcements and financial results (psx.com.pk)`,`Federal Board of Revenue: current withholding tax rates card (fbr.gov.pk)`],
  title:`Dividend yield: what it tells you and what it does not`,
  lede:`A high dividend yield can look like easy income. Understanding how it is calculated, and what can make it misleading, helps you read PSX stocks more carefully.`,
  body:`<h2>The financial question</h2><p>A friend says: "This PSX stock pays a 14% dividend yield. That is better than a savings account." Is that comparison fair? What does the yield actually tell you?</p><h2>The basic concept</h2><p>A <b>dividend</b> is a share of a company's profit paid in cash to shareholders. In Pakistan, companies usually announce dividends per share in rupees, or as a percentage of the share's <b>face value</b> (often 10 PKR). A "50% dividend" on a 10 PKR face value share means 5 PKR per share, not 50% of the market price.</p><p><b>Dividend yield</b> is the yearly dividend per share divided by the current market price, shown as a percentage:</p><p><b>Dividend yield = Annual dividend per share ÷ Share price × 100</b></p><h2>Why it matters to an ordinary Pakistani investor</h2><p>Many retail investors in Pakistan look for regular income from shares, especially when they compare stocks with bank deposits or National Savings schemes. Dividend yield is one way to make that comparison. It is useful, but only if you understand what sits behind the number.</p><p>It also matters because of inflation. If your after-tax dividend income is lower than inflation, the buying power of that income is shrinking, even if the rupee amount looks steady. And unlike a bank deposit, the value of the shares themselves can fall.</p><h2>The numbers and facts to understand</h2><ul><li><b>Dividend per share (DPS):</b> the cash paid per share over a year. Some companies pay once; others pay interim and final dividends.</li><li><b>Share price:</b> changes every trading day, so the yield changes too.</li><li><b>Payout ratio:</b> dividends as a percentage of profit (earnings per share, or EPS). A 60% payout means 60% of profit was paid out.</li><li><b>Tax:</b> dividends in Pakistan are taxed at source, and rates differ by type of company and by whether you are on the Active Taxpayers List. Rates change through annual budgets, so check the current FBR withholding tax card rather than relying on old figures.</li></ul><h2>How to analyse it, step by step</h2><ol><li>Find the total cash dividends per share paid over the last 12 months, from the company's announcements on the PSX website.</li><li>Divide by the current share price and multiply by 100.</li><li>Look at the last five years of dividends: steady, rising, falling or irregular?</li><li>Calculate the payout ratio: DPS ÷ EPS. Above 100% means the company paid out more than it earned that year.</li><li>Check cash from operations in the financial statements. Dividends are paid in cash, so cash generation matters.</li><li>Ask why the yield is high: rising dividends, or a falling share price?</li><li>Compare with similar companies in the same sector. Banks, fertiliser, power and cement companies often have very different dividend habits, so a sector comparison is more meaningful than a market-wide one.</li></ol><h2>A worked example (hypothetical numbers)</h2><p><b>Assumptions:</b> Company X shares trade at 80 PKR. Last year it paid 6 PKR per share. EPS was 10 PKR. You invest 100,000 PKR. We assume a 15% withholding tax on dividends for this example only; your actual rate may differ.</p><ul><li><b>Calculation:</b> Yield = 6 ÷ 80 × 100 = 7.5%.</li><li><b>Shares bought:</b> 100,000 ÷ 80 = 1,250 shares (ignoring brokerage).</li><li><b>Gross dividend:</b> 1,250 × 6 = 7,500 PKR.</li><li><b>After assumed tax:</b> 7,500 − 1,125 = 6,375 PKR, or about 6.4% on your money.</li><li><b>Payout ratio:</b> 6 ÷ 10 = 60%.</li></ul><p>Now suppose the price falls to 50 PKR because profits collapse and EPS drops to 4 PKR. The old dividend of 6 PKR would show a yield of 12%, which looks attractive. But the company now earns less than it paid last year, so the next dividend may be cut.</p><p><b>Interpretation:</b> the higher yield came from a falling price, not a better business. This is often called a <b>yield trap</b>.</p><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Confusing a dividend percentage of face value with dividend yield.</li><li>Using one unusually large, one-off dividend as if it repeats every year.</li><li>Ignoring the share price change. A 7% yield does not help much if the price falls 20%.</li><li>Forgetting tax and brokerage costs.</li><li>Buying just before the book closure date only to collect the dividend. Share prices usually adjust down by roughly the dividend amount after the stock goes ex-dividend, so there is no free gain.</li><li>Putting too much money into a few high-yield stocks from one sector, which concentrates risk.</li><li>Comparing a share's yield directly with a bank's profit rate without considering risk. Bank deposits have different risks from shares; share dividends are not guaranteed.</li></ul><h2>What dividend yield does NOT tell you</h2><ul><li>Whether the dividend will continue.</li><li>Whether the share price will rise or fall.</li><li>Whether the business is growing or shrinking.</li><li>Whether the company has too much debt.</li><li>Your total return, which is dividends plus or minus price change, after costs and inflation.</li></ul><h2>A simple checklist</h2><ol><li>Dividends per share for the last five years, written down</li><li>Current yield, calculated yourself</li><li>Payout ratio below 100% in most years</li><li>Cash from operations covering dividends</li><li>A reason for any sudden jump in yield</li><li>Current tax rate checked from an official source</li><li>Position size you can afford to see fall</li></ol><h2>Key takeaway</h2><p><b>A dividend yield is a starting question, not an answer. Always ask where the yield came from and whether the business can keep paying it.</b></p><p class='disclaimer'>Educational content only. Hypothetical numbers for illustration. Not a recommendation to buy or sell any security. Dividends are not guaranteed and share prices can fall, including below what you paid.</p>`},
 {slug:`fomo-buying-after-the-run`,pillar:`Mind`,date:`2026-10-04`,read:5,
  title:`FOMO: why we buy after the price has already run`,
  lede:`Fear of missing out pushes people to buy when everyone is talking about a stock or coin, which is often after most of the rise has happened. Here is how the pattern works and how to break it.`,
  body:`<h2>How the pattern looks</h2><p>A share or coin rises for weeks. WhatsApp groups, TikTok and friends start talking about it. You did not buy earlier, so now it feels urgent. You buy near the top, the price cools, and you sell in frustration. Then the cycle repeats with the next hot name.</p><h2>Why it happens</h2><ul><li><b>Social proof.</b> When many people do something, our brain reads it as safe and correct.</li><li><b>Regret avoidance.</b> The pain of having missed a gain feels personal, so we act to stop the feeling, not because of the facts.</li><li><b>Recent prices feel like the future.</b> A strong recent run makes it easy to assume it will continue.</li></ul><h2>The cost</h2><p>Buying late usually means paying a higher price with less room to rise and more room to fall. It also means you are acting on someone else's timing, often without knowing why they bought or when they will sell.</p><h2>Breaking the pattern</h2><ol><li><b>Write a rule:</b> "I do not buy anything within 48 hours of first hearing about it." The urge often fades; the good ideas are still there.</li><li><b>Ask three questions:</b> What does this business or asset actually do? What price would make it a bad buy? How much could I lose without harm?</li><li><b>Size small.</b> If you still want in, start with a small position you are comfortable losing.</li><li><b>Check the source.</b> Tips from unknown groups are not research. In Pakistan, check that anyone giving investment advice for a fee is licensed by the SECP.</li><li><b>Keep a "missed" list.</b> Note the ideas you skipped and review them later. You will find many that fell after the noise.</li></ol><div class='callout'><b>Pattern to remember.</b> When it feels most urgent to buy, slow down the most.</div><p class='disclaimer'>Educational content about behaviour, not financial advice or a recommendation on any security or asset.</p>`},
 {slug:`sunk-cost-trap`,pillar:`Mind`,date:`2026-10-04`,read:4,
  title:`The sunk cost trap: why we hold on to bad decisions`,
  lede:`Time, money or effort already spent cannot be recovered. Yet we often keep going with a bad plan just because we have already invested in it.`,
  body:`<h2>What a sunk cost is</h2><p>A sunk cost is anything you have already spent and cannot get back. The <b>sunk cost trap</b> is letting that past spending decide what you do next, instead of looking only at what is ahead.</p><h2>Everyday examples</h2><ul><li>Holding a losing share for years because selling would "make the loss real".</li><li>Staying in a course, job or business plan that clearly is not working because of the years already given to it.</li><li>Finishing a gym membership plan you hate instead of switching to walking or home training that you would actually do.</li><li>Eating a full plate when you are already full because "the food was paid for".</li></ul><h2>Why we fall for it</h2><p>People tend to feel losses more strongly than equal gains; researchers call this <b>loss aversion</b>. Stopping feels like admitting a loss, so we continue. Pride and what others will think add to the pressure.</p><h2>A better question</h2><p>Ask: <b>"If I were starting fresh today, with what I know now, would I choose this?"</b> If the answer is no, the money or time already spent is not a good reason to continue.</p><h2>Practical steps</h2><ol><li>Before starting something, write an exit rule: a price, a date or a result that means you stop.</li><li>Review big commitments on a schedule, for example every three months.</li><li>Separate the decision from the ego. Changing course with new information is a skill, not a failure.</li></ol><div class='callout'><b>Try this today.</b> Pick one thing you are continuing mainly because of what you already put in. Ask the fresh-start question and write your honest answer.</div>`},
 {slug:`inflation-explained`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-10-03`,read:5,
  sources:[`Pakistan Bureau of Statistics: Consumer Price Index releases (pbs.gov.pk)`,`State Bank of Pakistan: inflation monitor (sbp.org.pk)`],
  title:`Inflation explained: why cash under the mattress loses value`,
  lede:`Inflation is the rise in prices over time. It quietly shrinks what your savings can buy, which is why saving alone is not a plan.`,
  body:`<h2>What inflation is</h2><p>Inflation is the general rise in the prices of goods and services. In Pakistan it is measured by the Consumer Price Index (CPI), published by the Pakistan Bureau of Statistics. When people say inflation is 10%, they mean a typical basket of goods costs about 10% more than a year earlier.</p><h2>What it does to your savings</h2><p>Suppose you keep 100,000 PKR in cash for a year and inflation is 10%. You still have 100,000 PKR, but it now buys roughly what about 91,000 PKR bought a year ago. Nothing was stolen. Prices simply moved.</p><div class='tablewrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Inflation per year</th><th>What 100,000 PKR buys after 5 years (in today's prices)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label='Inflation per year'>5%</td><td data-label='After 5 years'>about 78,000 PKR</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Inflation per year'>10%</td><td data-label='After 5 years'>about 62,000 PKR</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Inflation per year'>20%</td><td data-label='After 5 years'>about 40,000 PKR</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>Nominal and real returns</h2><p>The <b>nominal</b> return is the number your bank or broker shows. The <b>real</b> return is what is left after inflation. A rough check is nominal return minus inflation. A savings account paying 12% in a year with 10% inflation leaves a real gain of about 2%. If the account pays 8%, you are losing about 2% a year in buying power.</p><h2>What people do about it</h2><ul><li>Keep only the cash you need for spending and emergencies.</li><li>Put long-term money in assets that have a chance of growing faster than prices, such as businesses (shares) or, for part of it, gold. Each carries its own risks.</li><li>Grow your income. Skills and earnings are the most reliable defence against rising prices.</li></ul><div class='callout'><b>Try this.</b> Look up the latest CPI figure and the profit rate on your own savings account. Subtract one from the other. That is your real return.</div><p class='disclaimer'>Educational content only. Figures are rounded illustrations, not forecasts. Not financial advice.</p>`},
 {slug:`emergency-fund-first`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-10-03`,read:4,
  title:`Build an emergency fund before you invest`,
  lede:`An emergency fund is money set aside for surprises: a job loss, a medical bill, a broken bike. It keeps one bad month from becoming a bad year.`,
  body:`<h2>Why it comes first</h2><p>Without a cushion, any surprise forces you to borrow or to sell investments, often at a bad price. With one, you can keep investing calmly when markets fall, because you do not need that money this month.</p><h2>How much</h2><p>A common guide is three to six months of essential expenses: rent, food, bills, transport, school fees and loan payments. If your income is irregular, or you support a large family, aim for the higher end.</p><h2>Where to keep it</h2><ul><li>Somewhere safe and easy to reach within a day or two.</li><li>Separate from your daily spending account, so it is not spent by accident.</li><li>Common choices are a bank savings account or a money market fund. Check how quickly you can withdraw and whether there are charges.</li></ul><p>It is not the place for stocks or crypto. Those can fall exactly when you need the money.</p><h2>How to build it</h2><ol><li>Add up one month of essential costs.</li><li>Set a first target of one month, then three, then six.</li><li>Set an automatic transfer on payday, even a small one.</li><li>After using it, refill it before starting new investments.</li></ol><div class='callout'><b>Small start.</b> If six months feels impossible, begin with 10,000 PKR. The habit matters more than the first amount.</div><p class='disclaimer'>General education, not personal financial advice.</p>`},
 {slug:`protein-pakistani-plate`,pillar:`Body`,date:`2026-10-03`,read:5,
  sources:[`US National Academies, Dietary Reference Intakes for protein (RDA 0.8 g/kg)`,`Jäger R et al. (2017). International Society of Sports Nutrition position stand: protein and exercise. JISSN`],
  title:`Protein on a Pakistani plate: how much and where from`,
  lede:`Protein helps your body build and repair muscle. You do not need special products to get enough. Everyday desi food can do the job.`,
  body:`<h2>How much</h2><p>A widely used baseline for adults is about 0.8 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight per day. People who train regularly often aim higher, commonly in the range of 1.2 to 2.0 grams per kilogram. A 70 kg adult who lifts weights might aim for roughly 85 to 140 grams a day.</p><h2>Everyday sources (approximate)</h2><div class='tablewrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Food</th><th>Serving</th><th>Protein</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label='Food'>Eggs</td><td data-label='Serving'>1 large egg</td><td data-label='Protein'>about 6 g</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Food'>Chicken</td><td data-label='Serving'>100 g cooked</td><td data-label='Protein'>about 25 to 30 g</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Food'>Daal (lentils)</td><td data-label='Serving'>1 cup cooked</td><td data-label='Protein'>about 15 to 18 g</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Food'>Chana (chickpeas)</td><td data-label='Serving'>1 cup cooked</td><td data-label='Protein'>about 14 g</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Food'>Dahi (yogurt)</td><td data-label='Serving'>1 cup</td><td data-label='Protein'>about 8 to 10 g</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Food'>Milk</td><td data-label='Serving'>1 glass (250 ml)</td><td data-label='Protein'>about 8 g</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Food'>Fish</td><td data-label='Serving'>100 g cooked</td><td data-label='Protein'>about 20 to 25 g</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>Spread it across the day</h2><p>Instead of one large meat meal at night, include some protein at each meal: eggs or dahi at breakfast, daal or chicken at lunch, and a mix at dinner.</p><h2>Vegetarian? Combine</h2><p>Daal with roti or rice, and chana with dahi, give a fuller mix of amino acids than either food alone.</p><div class='callout'><b>Easy upgrade.</b> Add two eggs or a bowl of dahi to the meal where you currently eat the least protein.</div><p>If you have kidney disease or another medical condition, ask a doctor before raising your protein intake.</p>`},
 {slug:`how-habits-form`,pillar:`Mind`,date:`2026-10-03`,read:4,
  sources:[`Lally P et al. (2010). How are habits formed: modelling habit formation in the real world. European Journal of Social Psychology`],
  title:`How habits form: cue, routine, reward`,
  lede:`A habit is a behaviour that runs with little thought. Understanding its three parts makes it easier to build good ones and break bad ones.`,
  body:`<h2>The loop</h2><ol><li><b>Cue.</b> Something that triggers the behaviour: a time, a place, a feeling, or the action before it.</li><li><b>Routine.</b> The behaviour itself.</li><li><b>Reward.</b> What you get from it, such as relief, energy, pleasure or a sense of progress.</li></ol><p>Repeat the loop often enough in the same setting and the behaviour starts to run more automatically.</p><h2>How long it takes</h2><p>A well-known study from University College London (Lally and colleagues, 2010) followed people building new daily habits. On average it took about 66 days for a behaviour to feel automatic, with a very wide range from 18 to 254 days. Missing a single day did not undo progress.</p><h2>Building a good habit</h2><ul><li><b>Attach it to an existing cue.</b> "After Fajr, I walk for ten minutes." "After I pour my tea, I write my three tasks."</li><li><b>Make it small.</b> Two push-ups, one page, one rupee saved. Grow it later.</li><li><b>Make the reward visible.</b> Tick a calendar or keep a streak.</li></ul><h2>Breaking a bad habit</h2><ul><li>Remove or hide the cue: phone out of the bedroom, snacks out of sight.</li><li>Replace the routine but keep a similar reward: a walk instead of scrolling when stressed.</li><li>Add friction: log out of the app, keep the card at home.</li></ul><div class='callout'><b>Write one line.</b> "After ______, I will ______." Fill it in for one habit and keep it for the next two months.</div>`},
 {slug:`gold-in-pakistan-basics`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-10-02`,read:5,
  title:`Buying gold in Pakistan: tola, karat and hidden costs`,
  lede:`Gold is a long-standing store of value in Pakistani families. Before you buy, understand the units, the purity and the costs that sit between the rate and your price.`,
  body:`<h2>The units</h2><p>Gold rates in Pakistan are usually quoted per <b>tola</b>. One tola is about 11.66 grams. Rates are also quoted per 10 grams, so check which one you are reading.</p><h2>Purity: karat</h2><div class='tablewrap'><table><thead><tr><th>Karat</th><th>Gold content</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td data-label='Karat'>24K</td><td data-label='Gold content'>about 99.9%</td><td data-label='Typical use'>Bars and coins</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Karat'>22K</td><td data-label='Gold content'>about 91.6%</td><td data-label='Typical use'>Most jewellery</td></tr><tr><td data-label='Karat'>21K</td><td data-label='Gold content'>about 87.5%</td><td data-label='Typical use'>Some jewellery</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Ask for the purity in writing on the receipt.</p><h2>The costs between the rate and your price</h2><ul><li><b>Making charges.</b> Jewellery includes a charge for the work, which you usually do not get back when selling.</li><li><b>Resale deduction.</b> Many jewellers deduct a percentage when buying gold back.</li><li><b>Buy and sell spread.</b> The rate a dealer sells at is higher than the rate they buy at.</li></ul><p>For saving, plain bars or coins from a reputable dealer usually lose less to these costs than jewellery.</p><h2>What moves the price</h2><p>The rupee price of gold depends on two things: the global gold price in US dollars and the rupee-to-dollar exchange rate. When the rupee weakens, gold in rupees tends to rise even if the global price is flat.</p><h2>What gold does not do</h2><p>Gold pays no profit or dividend. Its return comes only from price changes, and prices can fall for years. Many people treat it as one part of their savings, not all of it.</p><div class='callout'><b>Before you buy.</b> Check the day's rate, ask the purity, ask the making charge and ask the buy-back deduction. Write all four down.</div><p class='disclaimer'>Educational content only. Not financial advice.</p>`},
 {slug:`hydration-in-the-heat`,pillar:`Body`,date:`2026-10-02`,read:4,
  sources:[`World Health Organization: oral rehydration salts guidance`,`NHS: Heat exhaustion and heatstroke`],
  title:`Water, salt and heat: staying hydrated in a Pakistani summer`,
  lede:`In hot weather you lose water and salt through sweat. Replacing both, and knowing the warning signs of heat illness, protects your health and your training.`,
  body:`<h2>What you lose in sweat</h2><p>Sweat is mostly water, but it also carries electrolytes, mainly sodium. After long outdoor work or a hard session in the heat, plain water replaces the water but not the salt.</p><h2>Simple daily habits</h2><ul><li>Drink regularly through the day rather than a lot at once.</li><li>Check your urine: pale yellow usually means you are drinking enough.</li><li>Eat water-rich food such as cucumber, watermelon and dahi.</li><li>Train in the early morning or after sunset in peak summer.</li></ul><h2>When to use ORS</h2><p>Oral rehydration salts (ORS) are a measured mix of salts and sugar. They are useful after heavy sweating, diarrhoea or vomiting. Mix them exactly as the packet says. Lassi with a pinch of salt or nimbu pani with a little salt and sugar are traditional options for everyday heat.</p><h2>Warning signs of heat illness</h2><p><b>Heat exhaustion:</b> heavy sweating, weakness, dizziness, headache, nausea. Move to shade, cool down and drink fluids.</p><p><b>Heatstroke is an emergency:</b> very high body temperature, confusion, fainting, or hot dry skin. Call for medical help at once and cool the person with water and fanning while you wait.</p><div class='callout'><b>Before a summer workout.</b> Drink a glass of water, carry a bottle, and keep an ORS sachet in your bag.</div><p>People with heart or kidney conditions, or on certain medicines, should ask a doctor about fluid and salt intake.</p>`},
 {slug:`discipline-vs-motivation`,pillar:`Mind`,date:`2026-10-02`,read:4,
  title:`Discipline vs motivation: why systems beat feelings`,
  lede:`Motivation is a feeling that comes and goes. Discipline is a decision made in advance. If your progress depends on feeling motivated, it will be irregular.`,
  body:`<h2>Motivation is unreliable</h2><p>Motivation rises after a good video, a new year or a bad day you want to forget. It falls when you are tired, busy or bored. That is normal, not a personal failure.</p><h2>Discipline is mostly design</h2><p>Disciplined people rarely rely on willpower all day. They set up their day so the right action is the easy one: clothes laid out the night before, a fixed time for work, fewer choices to make.</p><h2>Four ways to build it</h2><ol><li><b>Decide the night before.</b> Write tomorrow's one main task and one exercise session, with times.</li><li><b>Shrink the start.</b> Commit only to beginning: open the book, put on the shoes. Starting is the hardest part.</li><li><b>Never miss twice.</b> One missed day is life. Two in a row is the start of a new habit.</li><li><b>Track, do not judge.</b> A simple tick chart shows patterns without guilt.</li></ol><h2>Where motivation still helps</h2><p>Use high-motivation days to set up systems: plan the week, prepare meals, automate a savings transfer. The system then carries you on low days.</p><div class='callout'><b>This week.</b> Choose one action you keep postponing. Give it a fixed day and time and make the first step take less than two minutes.</div>`},
 {slug:`longevity-basics`,pillar:`Body`,date:`2026-10-01`,read:5,
  title:`Longevity basics: the habits that matter most`,
  lede:`Living longer and staying capable is built from a short list of ordinary habits, repeated for decades. Here is the list, without the hype.`,
  body:`<h2>Move every week</h2><p>The World Health Organization recommends adults aim for at least 150 to 300 minutes of moderate activity per week, such as brisk walking, plus muscle-strengthening work on two or more days. Strength matters for staying independent as you age.</p><h2>Protect your sleep</h2><p>A steady wake-up time, daylight in the morning and a calm last hour of the evening are simple supports. Many adults do best with around seven to nine hours.</p><h2>Eat mostly simple food</h2><p>Build meals around vegetables, fruit, pulses, whole grains and enough protein. Go lighter on sugary drinks and heavily processed snacks.</p><h2>Avoid the big known harms</h2><p>Not smoking is among the most effective choices for long-term health. Keep alcohol low or avoid it.</p><h2>Stay connected and keep a purpose</h2><p>Regular time with family and friends, and work or projects that matter to you, are part of a healthy long life.</p><div class='callout'><b>Get the checks done.</b> Blood pressure, blood sugar and other screenings are part of the picture. Ask a doctor which ones suit your age and history.</div><p>Nobody can promise a number of years from any habit. These are the basics that health bodies broadly agree on, and they work best when started early and kept simple.</p>`},
 {slug:`longevity-and-money`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-10-01`,read:4,
  title:`If you may live to 90, your money has to last`,
  lede:`A longer life is good news that comes with a bill. Planning for it starts with time, not with a large amount.`,
  body:`<h2>Time does the heavy lifting</h2><p>Compounding means returns earn their own returns. If you invest 10,000 PKR a month at an assumed 10% yearly return, you would have roughly 2.0 million PKR after 10 years and about 7.6 million after 20. The assumed return is only an illustration, and real returns vary and can be negative.</p><h2>Plan for a long retirement</h2><ul><li>Assume you may need income for 25 to 30 years after you stop working.</li><li>Allow for inflation, which reduces what each rupee buys over time.</li><li>Keep an emergency fund so one shock does not force you to sell investments at a bad moment.</li></ul><h2>Your health is part of the plan</h2><p>Medical costs often rise with age. Staying active and keeping up your health checks is also a financial decision.</p><div class='callout'><b>One step this week.</b> Set an automatic monthly transfer, even a small one, on the day you get paid.</div><p class='disclaimer'>Illustrative arithmetic only. Not financial advice. Returns are not guaranteed and capital can be lost.</p>`},
 {slug:`what-is-psx-alpha`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-10-01`,read:4,
  title:`What PSX Alpha is, and how this blog covers the market`,
  lede:`PSX Alpha is a method for researching Pakistan Stock Exchange companies: read the facts, write down your reasoning, and size every position before you enter.`,
  body:`<h2>The idea</h2><p>The Pakistan Stock Exchange (PSX) has hundreds of listed companies, daily news and a lot of loud opinion. PSX Alpha is a way to cut through that. It is a set of habits for a retail investor, not a tip service and not a prediction engine.</p><h2>The three parts</h2><ol><li><b>Read the facts.</b> Company announcements, financial results, policy rate decisions and the daily index move. These are the inputs worth your time.</li><li><b>Write the reasoning.</b> For every stock you consider, write one paragraph: what the business earns, what could go wrong, and what would make you change your mind.</li><li><b>Size before you enter.</b> Decide how much you can lose on the position before you decide to buy it.</li></ol><div class='callout'><b>What you will find here.</b> Posts on reading a daily market brief, the basic numbers behind a company, the difference between an index and your own portfolio, and risk management. Posts are educational and describe methods, not recommendations.</div><h2>Who it is for</h2><p>Long-horizon investors and traders in Pakistan who want a repeatable process. If you are new, start with the posts tagged Money and read them in order.</p><p class='disclaimer'>This is a research method, not a recommendation to buy or sell any security. Markets carry risk, including the loss of capital.</p>`},
 {slug:`five-numbers-before-a-psx-stock`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-09-30`,read:6,
  title:`Five numbers to check before you read about any PSX stock`,
  lede:`Before you read an opinion on a company, look at five figures from its own financial statements. They tell you what kind of business it is.`,
  body:`<h2>1. Earnings per share (EPS) and its trend</h2><p>EPS is profit after tax divided by the number of shares. One year says little. Look at the last five years and ask whether it is rising, falling or jumping around.</p><h2>2. Price-to-earnings (P/E)</h2><p>P/E is the share price divided by EPS. A share priced at 100 with EPS of 10 has a P/E of 10. Compare it with the company’s own history and with similar companies in the same sector, not with the whole market.</p><h2>3. Dividend yield and payout</h2><p>Dividend yield is the yearly dividend per share divided by the share price. Also check the payout: the share of profit paid out. A high yield paid from falling profit may not last.</p><h2>4. Debt against equity</h2><p>Debt-to-equity compares what the company owes with what its owners have put in. High debt makes profit more fragile when interest rates are high. Read the interest expense line in the results.</p><h2>5. Cash from operations</h2><p>Profit is an accounting figure. Cash from operations shows whether the business actually brings in cash. Profit that never turns into cash deserves a question.</p><div class='callout'><b>Quick routine.</b> Open the latest annual report, write the five numbers on one line each, then write one sentence on what they say together.</div><p class='disclaimer'>These are definitions and a reading method. They are not a recommendation to buy or sell any security.</p>`},
 {slug:`index-is-not-your-portfolio`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-09-29`,read:5,
  title:`The KSE-100 is not your portfolio`,
  lede:`The index is a weighted list of large companies. Your results depend on what you hold, when you bought and how much you put into each position.`,
  body:`<h2>What the index measures</h2><p>The KSE-100 tracks 100 companies on the PSX, weighted by the market value of their free-floating shares. Larger companies move it more. If a few heavy names rise, the index can rise while many other stocks do not.</p><h2>Why your numbers differ</h2><ul><li><b>Different holdings.</b> You own a handful of stocks, not the full list.</li><li><b>Different weights.</b> Your position sizes are your own.</li><li><b>Different timing.</b> Your purchase dates set your return, not the index’s start date.</li><li><b>Dividends and costs.</b> Brokerage, taxes and dividends change what you actually keep.</li></ul><h2>Measure in real terms</h2><p>Inflation reduces what a rupee buys. A rough check is nominal return minus inflation. If your portfolio rose 15% in a year and inflation was 10%, your real gain is roughly 5%.</p><div class='callout'><b>Do this once a quarter.</b> Record your portfolio value, the index level and the inflation rate on the same date. Compare changes, not levels.</div><p class='disclaimer'>Educational content only. Not financial advice. Past results do not predict future results.</p>`},
 {slug:`why-one-framework`,pillar:`Mind`,date:`2026-09-29`,read:4,
  title:`Why mind, body and money belong in one framework`,
  lede:`Each of the three supports the others. A tired body weakens your decisions, a stressed mind weakens your training, and money pressure weakens both.`,
  body:`<h2>The three pillars</h2><ul><li><b>Mind:</b> attention, discipline and how you make decisions.</li><li><b>Body:</b> movement, food and sleep.</li><li><b>Money:</b> earning, saving and investing with a plan.</li></ul><h2>How they connect</h2><p>When you sleep badly, small decisions get harder. When you are anxious about money, it is harder to keep a training routine. When you have a routine, you have more energy for the work that earns. None of this is a promise. It is an observation worth testing on yourself.</p><h2>The common thread: rules over moods</h2><p>All three pillars improve with the same method. Decide in advance, write it down, keep it small and review it on a schedule. That is what this blog calls a framework.</p><div class='callout'><b>Start with one line per pillar.</b> One habit for the mind, one for the body, one for money. Keep each so small that you cannot fail it.</div>`},
 {slug:`sunday-review`,pillar:`Mind`,date:`2026-09-28`,read:4,
  title:`The fifteen-minute Sunday review`,
  lede:`A short weekly review keeps all three pillars in view. It takes fifteen minutes and needs only a notebook.`,
  body:`<h2>The steps</h2><ol><li><b>Mind (5 min).</b> What did I plan this week, and what did I finish? Where did I lose focus?</li><li><b>Body (5 min).</b> How many sessions did I do? How were my sleep times and meals? Pick one thing to adjust.</li><li><b>Money (5 min).</b> What did I spend, save or invest? Did I follow my own rules on any trade or purchase? Note any decision I want to review.</li></ol><h2>Then set next week</h2><p>Write one action for each pillar, with a day and a time. Keep each action small enough to do on a bad day.</p><div class='callout'><b>Keep a running log.</b> After a month, read your own notes. The patterns show up in your handwriting, not in anyone’s advice.</div>`},
 {slug:`steady-sleep-and-wake-time`,pillar:`Body`,date:`2026-09-27`,read:4,
  title:`A steady wake-up time is the cheapest habit to build`,
  lede:`Training and food get most of the attention. A regular wake time costs nothing and gives the rest of your routine a fixed starting point.`,
  body:`<h2>Fix the wake time first</h2><p>Choose a wake-up time you can keep on most days, including weekends. A consistent start makes it easier to place everything else: exercise, meals and work.</p><h2>Simple supports</h2><ul><li>Get daylight soon after waking, even for a few minutes.</li><li>Keep screens out of the last part of the evening where you can.</li><li>Leave a gap between a heavy meal or caffeine and bedtime.</li><li>Keep the room dark and as cool as is comfortable.</li></ul><div class='callout'><b>Try this for two weeks.</b> Set one alarm, get up when it goes off, and note how you feel by day ten.</div><p>If you struggle with sleep for a long time, or feel very tired in the day, speak to a doctor. These are general habits, not treatment for any condition.</p>`},
 {slug:`one-page-trade-plan`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-09-26`,read:5,
  title:`The one-page trade plan: write it before you click buy`,
  lede:`A trade plan fits on one page and answers six questions. If you cannot answer them, you are not ready to enter.`,
  body:`<h2>The six lines</h2><ol><li><b>Idea.</b> One sentence on why this stock or coin, in plain words.</li><li><b>Entry.</b> The price or condition at which you act.</li><li><b>Stop.</b> The price at which the idea is proven wrong, and you exit.</li><li><b>Size.</b> The amount you will lose if the stop is hit, set as a small share of your account.</li><li><b>Target or review date.</b> Where you will take profit, or the date you will review the position.</li><li><b>What would change my mind.</b> A fact, not a feeling.</li></ol><div class='callout'><b>Why one page.</b> A short plan is easier to follow when the price is moving. If the plan is long, you will stop reading it.</div><h2>After the trade</h2><p>Add two lines: what happened, and whether you followed the plan. Judge yourself on the second line first. A loss that followed the plan is a normal cost of trading. A win that broke the plan is a habit to fix.</p><p class='disclaimer'>A method, not a recommendation to buy or sell any security or asset. Trading carries risk of loss.</p>`},
 {slug:`walk-every-day`,pillar:`Body`,date:`2026-09-25`,read:4,
  title:`Walking: the training habit with the lowest barrier`,
  lede:`You need no gym, no kit and no plan to start. A daily walk is the easiest habit to keep, and it counts toward weekly activity guidelines.`,
  body:`<h2>Why start here</h2><p>Brisk walking is a moderate activity. The World Health Organization suggests adults aim for 150 to 300 minutes of moderate activity a week. That is about 20 to 40 minutes a day.</p><h2>How to make it stick</h2><ul><li>Fix the time. After a meal or after morning prayer works well for many people.</li><li>Walk at a pace where you can talk but not sing.</li><li>Start with ten minutes and add five minutes each week.</li><li>Keep your phone in your pocket for part of the walk.</li></ul><div class='callout'><b>Make it count in your day.</b> Walk to a nearby errand, take calls on foot, or get off one stop early.</div><p>If you have a heart condition, joint pain or have been inactive for a long time, ask a doctor before increasing your activity.</p>`},
 {slug:`one-focused-block`,pillar:`Mind`,date:`2026-09-24`,read:4,
  title:`One focused 50-minute block a day`,
  lede:`Most of the work that moves your life forward needs uninterrupted attention. Protect one block each day for it.`,
  body:`<h2>The rules</h2><ol><li><b>Pick one task</b> the night before. One, not three.</li><li><b>Set a timer</b> for 50 minutes.</li><li><b>Remove the phone</b> from the room, or switch it off.</li><li><b>Stop when the timer ends,</b> even if you are in flow. Take a short break.</li></ol><h2>What to use it for</h2><p>Study, writing, a financial review, or learning a skill. Choose work that you would otherwise keep putting off.</p><div class='callout'><b>Judge the week, not the day.</b> Aim for five blocks across seven days. Skipping one day does not end the streak.</div>`},
 {slug:`crypto-and-psx-risk`,pillar:`Money`,date:`2026-09-23`,read:5,
  title:`Crypto and PSX need different risk rules`,
  lede:`Both are risky markets, but they behave differently. Your sizing and your hours should reflect that.`,
  body:`<h2>Differences that matter</h2><ul><li><b>Trading hours.</b> The PSX trades on weekdays in fixed sessions. Crypto trades all day, every day, so news can move prices while you sleep.</li><li><b>Volatility.</b> Crypto prices often move more in a day than a typical large PSX stock. Check recent daily ranges before choosing a size.</li><li><b>Rules and protections.</b> Listed Pakistani companies sit inside a regulated market. Crypto exchanges and coins vary widely in rules and risk.</li></ul><h2>What to change in practice</h2><ol><li>Use a smaller position size for the more volatile asset.</li><li>Place your stop and size before the trade, since you may not be watching.</li><li>Keep the total share of your savings in crypto at a level you could lose without harm.</li></ol><div class='callout'><b>One question.</b> If this position fell by half tonight, would my life change? If yes, the size is too big.</div><p class='disclaimer'>Educational content only. Not financial advice. You can lose some or all of your money in either market.</p>`},
 {slug:`strength-twice-a-week`,pillar:`Body`,date:`2026-09-22`,read:4,
  title:`Strength twice a week: a simple plan`,
  lede:`Muscle-strengthening on two or more days a week is part of standard adult activity guidelines. Five basic movements cover it.`,
  body:`<h2>The five movements</h2><ol><li><b>Squat or sit-to-stand</b> from a chair.</li><li><b>Push-up,</b> on the floor or against a wall.</li><li><b>Row,</b> with a backpack or a resistance band.</li><li><b>Hip hinge,</b> such as a good morning or a deadlift with a light weight.</li><li><b>Plank,</b> held with a straight line from head to heels.</li></ol><h2>How much</h2><p>Do two or three sets of 8 to 12 slow repetitions of each, on two non-consecutive days. When the last set feels easy, add a repetition or a little weight.</p><div class='callout'><b>Form before load.</b> Move slowly and stop a set when your form breaks down.</div><p>Stop if you feel sharp pain. If you have an injury or a health condition, check with a doctor or physiotherapist first.</p>`},
 {slug:`decision-journal`,pillar:`Mind`,date:`2026-09-21`,read:3,
  title:`A decision journal in three lines`,
  lede:`You cannot judge a decision by its result alone. Write down why you made it, then check later.`,
  body:`<h2>The three lines</h2><ol><li><b>Decision and date.</b> What did I choose?</li><li><b>Reason and state.</b> Why, and how was I feeling: calm, rushed, excited?</li><li><b>Review date.</b> When will I look again?</li></ol><h2>Why it works</h2><p>Memory edits itself. Without notes, you remember the decisions that worked and forget the reasoning behind the ones that did not. A written record lets you see patterns, such as rushed decisions turning out worse.</p><div class='callout'><b>Use it for money decisions first.</b> Every trade, large purchase or investment gets three lines before you act.</div>`}
];
